n  and just realize that really basketball  is the common story and it really unites  for this store we had to install bunk  beds outdoor showers  ac system we replaced the indoors with  hardwood floors  we changed the seating area so people  can face each other and it's more  conversational  for a project like this to come together  it's all about the people involved  the diversity amongst us is phenomenal  we have people from  every different background we've been  almost all the way across the country  now  starting off in l.a where the whole  buses originated venice beach california  hit redlands for a small protest palm  springs phoenix arizona  albuquerque new mexico dallas texas we  went to houston texas went to new  orleans on bourbon street batesville we  went to birmingham  atlanta georgia charlotte north carolina  raleigh north carolina then  washington dc this is an event that was  kind of announced through social media  even in small towns there's like 100 200  people people already know about the who  bus is  incredible it's already become just like  vehicle of change  one of the main things on our trip was  written proving courts  one of my favorite moments was in dallas  we built a court  for a community and it was phenomenal  like these guys really came to our  community  our little community where nobody comes  they talk to us they shook our hands and  they build a whole court for us  just to see the excitement and the joy  that the kids had  that makes an impact even after we leave  i mean we pull up the cities and we you  know play some basketball and we have  conversations and then we go to the next  city  but i think that we were able to really  make a lasting impact in that community  in oak cliff  being in atlanta has been amazing it was  definitely one of the spots when we were  looking across the map like where do we  want to stop it was atlanta 100  it's a huge huge culture spot for civil  rights  there's so much history in atlanta first  